Large language models (LLMs) are powerful at generating and understanding text. 
However, when an LLM is used in isolation, it faces several inherent limitations

  Limited knowledge beyond training
  No built-in access to external data
  Context window limitations


What makes a system agentic?
   Agentic systems provide an LLM with a set of external tools and access to dynamic context, 
   empowering the model to determine when and how to use those tools as it works to solve a task.
   In other words, the LLM is no longer just a passive generator of text; it is augmented with the capacity to:
   Retrieve information (for example, searching the web or querying a database)
   Invoke services or APIs (such as calling a weather service, sending an email, or running a calculation)
   Maintain longer context or memory (by storing and recalling conversation history or relevant documents as needed)

Crucially, the agent decides which tool to use and when, based on the problem it’s trying to solve. 
